# 5. Entity Model

Every entity inside the Universe follows the same rules.

Every entity must contain

```json
{
    "id":"",
    "name":"",
    "metadata":{}
}
```

The remaining fields depend on entity type.

---

## Shared Metadata

Every entity metadata object contains

```json
{
    "first_appearance":"",

    "last_appearance":"",

    "importance":0,

    "confidence":1.0
}
```

### first_appearance

Chapter or event where the entity first appears.

---

### last_appearance

Latest appearance in canon.

---

### importance

Relative importance.

Range

```
1–100
```

---

### confidence

Extraction confidence.

Range

```
0.0–1.0
```

Universe Builder assigns this.

Other engines ignore it.

---

# 6. Character Schema

Every fictional character is represented exactly once.

Characters are **canonical definitions**, not live instances.

Their mutable properties belong in World State.

Example

```json
{
    "id":"char_hermione",

    "name":"Hermione Granger",

    "aliases":[
        "Hermione"
    ],

    "description":"A brilliant Hogwarts student.",

    "personality":{},

    "speech":{},

    "goals":[],

    "morality":{},

    "abilities":[],

    "knowledge_scope":[],

    "metadata":{}
}
```

A Character stores who they are.

Not what they are doing.
---

## Character Fields

### id

Unique character identifier.

Must remain constant for the lifetime of the universe.

Format

```
char_<name>
```

Examples

```
char_harry

char_hermione

char_gandalf

char_eren
```

---

### name

Canonical character name.

Example

```
Hermione Granger
```

---

### aliases

Alternative names.

Example

```json
[
    "Hermione",
    "Miss Granger"
]
```

Useful for entity resolution.

---

### description

A concise canonical description.

Example

```
Brilliant witch and one of Harry Potter's closest friends.
```

This should describe the character,

not narrate events.

